.TH DSPOOLREFEED 8
.SH NAME
dspoolrefeed \- write dnewslink queue files for every article on the spool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dspoolrefeed
[
.B \-C
.I diablo.config
]
[
.B \-a
.I days
]
[
.B \-g
.I groups
]
[
.B \-n
.I lines
]
[
.B \-o
.I prefix
]
[
.B \-q
]
[
.B \-V
]
[
.I historyfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dspoolrefeed
reads the history file
(default: the
.B path_history
of
.BR diablo.config )
and writes one queue line for each article that is still on the spool,
in the form that
.BR diablo (8)
writes to its queue files:
.PP
.RS
D.xxxxxxxx/B.xxxx <message-id> offset,size L\fIlines\fR
.RE
.PP
with the number of body lines in
.B L\fIlines\fR
(which
.B dnewslink \-H
passes to a reader for its overview), and
.B C\fIsize\fR
at the end for an article on a compressed spool object.
Every article is read whole to count its lines.
.BR dnewslink (8)
then offers the articles to another server
(\fBCHECK\fR/\fBTAKETHIS\fR, or \fBIHAVE\fR),
for example to fill a new server, or one that lost its spool.
.PP
The history keeps only a hash of each Message-ID, so
.B dspoolrefeed
reads the Message-ID from the article's header on the spool, and checks
that it hashes to the history entry; an entry whose offset no longer holds
that article is left out.
Expired entries, header-only entries, and entries whose file or article is
gone are left out too.
At the end, the counts of written and left-out entries go to standard
error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a \fIdays\fR
Only articles received in the last
.I days
days.
.TP
.B \-g \fIgroups\fR
Only articles posted to a matching newsgroup.
.I groups
is a comma-separated list of wildmats; a pattern with a leading
.B !
excludes, and for each newsgroup the last matching pattern decides
(for example
.BR comp.*,!comp.binaries.* ).
An article is written when one of its newsgroups is selected.
A Newsgroups header longer than about 16 KB counts as not selected, and a
list longer than 4095 characters is refused.
.TP
.B \-n \fIlines\fR
With
.BR \-o ,
start a new file every
.I lines
entries, so several
.B dnewslink
processes can send the parts in parallel.
.TP
.B \-o \fIprefix\fR
Write the queue to the file
.I prefix
(with
.BR \-n :
\fIprefix\fR.0000, \fIprefix\fR.0001, ...)
instead of standard output.
An existing file is not overwritten.
.TP
.B \-q
Do not print the counts.
.TP
.B \-C \fIdiablo.config\fR
Use this configuration file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Refeed the last 30 days, in parts of 100000 articles, as the news user
from the queue directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd /news/dqueue
dspoolrefeed -a 30 -n 100000 -o refeed
for f in refeed.????; do
    dnewslink -h newpeer.example.com -b $f -D &
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B dnewslink
takes the
.B \-b
file name relative to the queue directory
.RB ( path_dqueue ),
so write the files there.
It rewrites each file with the entries the peer deferred, so running the
same command again retries them; articles the peer already has are
refused, and nothing is sent twice.
.SH NOTES
The receiving server must accept the old articles: with INN, raise
.B artcutoff
(default 10 days) for the time of the refeed, and give the sending host a
peer entry
.RB ( incoming.conf ).
Diablo checks the article age with
.B feedermaxacceptage
on the receiving side.
.PP
The history is read in its own order, not by time.
Sorting a queue file
.RB ( "sort -o refeed.0000 refeed.0000" )
groups the lines by spool directory, which sends the articles roughly in
the order they arrived and reads each spool file in one pass.
.PP
Whether a spool object is compressed is taken from the current
.BR dspool.ctl ,
as diablo itself reads its articles: run it with the
.B compresslvl
setting the articles were stored with.
.PP
On a large spool every article's header is read once; run it at a quiet
time, or limit it with
.B \-a
or
.BR \-g .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR diablo (8),
.BR dnewslink (8),
.BR didump (8),
.BR dreadart (8),
.BR dspoolout (8)
